...are combined and called 'hatthagahaṇaṃ'. Therefore, in the Padabhājanī of that term, it is said: "Should consent to the taking of the hand; 'hand' means from the elbow to the tips of the nails; for the sake of indulging in this ignoble practice (asaddhamma), if one consents to being taken above the collarbone and below the kneecap, there is a thullaccaya (grave) offense." In this context, 'asaddhamma' should be understood as physical contact (kāyasaṃsagga), not sexual intercourse (methunadhamma). This is because a thullaccaya offense does not occur in the vicinity of sexual intercourse. The statement "A wise [nun] is capable of falling into physical contact" is also evidence for this meaning.

"In three types of women, one should not indulge in that intercourse;
In three types of men and three types of ignoble eunuchs (paṇḍaka);
One should not practice intercourse in one's own organ;
Because of the cause of sexual intercourse, there would be a cutting off (from the Saṃgha);
This problem has been considered by the wise."

If it is asked whether this conflicts with the Sedamocaka verse mentioned in the Parivāra, it does not. This is because it is the preliminary stage (pubbabhāga) of sexual intercourse. In the Parivāra itself, it is said, "The preliminary stage of sexual intercourse should be known," and the five training rules—emission of semen (sukkhavissaṭṭhi), physical contact (kāyasaṃsagga), lewd words (duṭṭhullavācā), serving one's own lust (attakāmapāricariyā), and acting as a go-between (gamanuppādana)—are stated to be the preliminary stages of sexual intercourse. Therefore, physical contact is a cause [for Pārājika] because it is a preliminary stage of sexual intercourse. Thus, the meaning of "cutting off because of the cause of sexual intercourse" should be understood in this manner. By this method, the decision for all terms should be understood. Furthermore, in the Padabhājanī for the term "saṅketam vā gaccheyya" (or should go to a rendezvous), the meaning is "come to such-and-such a place."

Regarding the statement that a nun who completes the eighth item is no longer a nun (falls into Pārājika): whether in forward order, reverse order, or skipping one, only by completing the eighth item by any of those methods does she cease to be a nun. However, a nun who completes one item or seven items even a hundred times does not cease to be a nun. She is released by confessing the offenses incurred. Nevertheless, in this context, the offense that enters into the count (gaṇanūpikā) should be understood. For it is said: "There is a confessed offense that enters the count; there is a confessed offense that does not enter the count." In that regard, this is the decision: an offense confessed after giving up the burden [of committing] by saying "I will not offend now" enters the count; having reached the count of confessed [offenses], it is no longer a part (aṅga) of the Pārājika.
